### Muonic X-rays from a mu- beam in the 20 x 20 x 4 cm target lying on the LYSO panel, one job (2026-11-07, the beam study
### of Al in Si; condor/beam_xray_submit.sh writes and runs many). The setup of macro/muonic_xray_lyso.mac: the
### 126.88 x 126.88 x 3.18 mm LYSO panel of 20 x 20 pixels, its Al top face against the target's bottom face at 32.5 cm.
### The cosmic rays are replaced by a mono-energetic mu- beam straight down (-z) onto the target's top face (36.5 cm),
### starting at bmZ (cm), with a Gaussian spot of sigma bmSigX x bmSigY (cm; PSI muE1: 39 x 25 mm FWHM = 1.656 x 1.062 cm)
### and kinetic energy bmEkin (MeV). 29.95 MeV (85 MeV/c) stops at 2.5 cm depth in Si (rms 0.9 mm), 1.5 cm above the panel.
### Every radioactive decay of the nuclei that the captures leave behind is simulated, at its own time (minutes to years
### later); analysis/beam_impurity_spectrum.C weights such hits by their chance of falling inside the measuring time.
### The panel's own Lu-176 is not overlaid here (the gun mode has no overlay). At beam rates every Lu-176 entry counts, so
### the analysis adds them from the decay-only runs. Only events with energy in the panel and a muon stopped in the
### target are written (/EventFilter 8).
### The job macro sets the target (/tof/target/material, impurity, impurityFraction) and the aliases bmSeed, bmEvents,
### bmDir, bmFile, bmEkin, bmSigX, bmSigY and bmZ, then executes this. Needs TOFmeter --preinit macro/preinit_lyso.mac
### (radioactive decay and fluorescence).
/tof/det/worldSize 420 420 300 cm
/tof/det/stack false
/tof/target/size 20 20 4 cm
/tof/target/position 0 0 34.5 cm
/control/execute macro/panel_lyso.mac
/tof/czt/position 0 0 32.332 cm
/update
/OutputFormat 1
/EventFilter 8
/OutputDirectory {bmDir}
/OutputFile {bmFile}
/gun/seed {bmSeed}
/GeneralParticleSource 1
/gps/particle mu-
/gps/pos/type Beam
/gps/pos/centre 0 0 {bmZ} cm
/gps/pos/sigma_x {bmSigX} cm
/gps/pos/sigma_y {bmSigY} cm
/gps/direction 0 0 -1
/gps/ene/type Mono
/gps/ene/mono {bmEkin} MeV
# Auger electrons off (the radioactive decay turned them on), as in the LYSO cosmic runs: their few keV stay in the pixel
# anyway, and they make the run 1.5 times slower. Fluorescence (K X-rays that can leave a pixel) stays on.
/process/em/auger false
/process/em/augerCascade false
# verbose 1: the log ends with "Number of events processed : <bmEvents>", which beam_xray_job.sh and _check.sh look for
/run/verbose 1
/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn {bmEvents}
